⏺ How Voice-Node is Built

  One ECS Task = One Complete Phone System:

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │           One Voice-Node Task                    │
  │                                                  │
  │  ┌─────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌──────────┐ │
  │  │ SIP Server  │  │ Controller │  │ Recorder │ │
  │  │ (FreeSWITCH)│←→│ (Python)   │  │ (Java)   │ │
  │  │             │  │            │  │          │ │
  │  │ Makes/takes │  │ Brain -    │  │ Saves    │ │
  │  │ phone calls │  │ decides    │  │ call     │ │
  │  │             │  │ what to do │  │ audio    │ │
  │  └─────────────┘  └────────────┘  └──────────┘ │
  │                                                  │
  │  Shared folder: /opt/ccs-voice                   │
  │  All talk via localhost (host networking)         │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  Production runs 4 of these, spread across different availability zones.

  ---
  How Calls Work

  1. Call comes in via NLB (Network Load Balancer) → hits one SIP server
  2. SIP Server tells Controller "new call arrived"
  3. Controller talks to Flows engine ("what script to run?"), speech services, chatbot, etc.
  4. Controller keeps all call state in a Python dictionary in memory
  5. When call ends, Controller writes CDR (call detail record) and done

  ---
  What's Designed Well

  - Host networking — needed for 10,000 UDP ports (voice media)
  - distinctInstance placement — each task gets its own EC2 box (no resource contention)
  - 5400s stop timeout — during planned deploys, active calls get 1.5 hours to finish
  - Health check (GET /check) — if FreeSWITCH dies, NLB stops sending traffic
  - Graceful shutdown on SIGTERM — handles planned ECS rolling updates fine
  - CloudWatch alarms — 4xx/5xx errors, unhealthy hosts → ServiceNow alerts

  ---
  What's Missing

  ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │                         Gap                         │                        Why it matters                         │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No /drain endpoint                                  │ Nothing external can say "stop taking calls and save state"   │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ Call state only in memory                           │ Server dies = all scheduled calls vanish forever              │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No Spot interruption handling                       │ AWS gives 2-min warning but nobody listens for it             │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No distinction: Spot vs On-Demand for stateful work │ Stateful voice tasks shouldn't be on interruptible instances  │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ No auto-recovery for scheduled calls                │ Other nodes can't pick up what a dead node was supposed to do │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  ---
  The Gap in One Sentence

  The system handles planned shutdowns perfectly (rolling deploy, maintenance) but has zero protection for unplanned deaths (Spot reclaim, OOM crash) — and that's where clients lose calls.



Voice Node Service — Implementation Plan

 Context

 Building a FastAPI-based Voice Node service for a test web application that handles plant delivery sampling. The app processes delivery steps and makes outbound calls to inform recipients of step completion.
 Calls use AWS Polly (TTS) and Transcribe (STT), with DTMF handling for recipient actions.

 The service runs on ECS with EC2 (later Spot instances). When a node drains (spot interruption), incomplete calls are NOT marked as done — they stay pending so the next ECS task retries with all original
 parameters.

 Project path: /Users/ssrivastava/voice-node

